SEMANTIC-DIFF SD-PROCESS-1-no-truncation-flag blocking=YES approval=PENDING — stdout/stderr are silently TAIL-truncated at the declared 262144-byte cap and the record carries no truncation flag, so a consumer cannot distinguish "the command printed exactly this" from "the head was dropped" (PB-B4: 300022 emitted bytes arrive as exactly 262144 ending with the final marker)
SEMANTIC-DIFF SD-PROCESS-2-killed-exit-status blocking=YES approval=PENDING — a child killed by the caller timeout reports exitCode=null plus a SIGNAL name; the status is not a number and the signal name is excluded from the golden by the printed exclusion set, so a new path must agree on the null-status shape and the caller-visible settle flags (timedOut) rather than on a numeric exit
SEMANTIC-DIFF SD-PROCESS-3-pty-face-not-measured blocking=YES approval=PENDING — the shipped product wiring mounts NO governed PTY face, so the measured `bash` is the route-C `confined-bash-tool.mjs` (PD-12) definition over the confined subprocess face; `native-bash-tool.mjs` (NATIVE-BASH-PRESERVATION, the pinned host `bash-persistent` definition) is selected whenever a composition wires `faces.terminals.governedPty`, and that face adds persistent `cd`/`export` state and the `stty -echo`/marker protocol this corpus does NOT cover
SEMANTIC-DIFF SD-PROCESS-4-refusal-surface blocking=YES approval=PENDING — lane denials are typed `IO_READ_DENIED` with authored-locator prose ("authored locator '…' is outside every allow subtree of the lane"), and a missing workdir shares the SAME code with different prose; the corpus compares a closed CATEGORY vocabulary and the filesystem witness, never the code or the sentence, so a new path that answers a different code is compatible only by an approved semantic change
SEMANTIC-DIFF SD-PROCESS-5-pwsh-member-unreachable blocking=YES approval=PENDING — the census books two members of `dsh.process-family` (`bash`, `pwsh`), but `pwsh` is unreachable on the product route (no route-C shadow, no preset row — the shipped governed preset REMOVES `tool-pwsh` as an un-instrumented second shell) and no `pwsh` binary exists on this machine; the unit is therefore covered 1 of 2, and a new path cannot claim the unit until a pwsh-capable composition and host are measured
